  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 219 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAt the start of the workweek on Monday, guidance consensus is that \nflow aloft over the Middle Mississippi Valley will be quazi-zonal as \na trough swings eastward through the Upper Midwest. At the surface, \nan expansive area of high pressure will continue oozing into the \nMidwest. Even with a lack of deep cold air advection over the \nregion, the post-frontal air mass itself will be cold enough to push \ntemperatures on Monday for much of the area below seasonal normals. \nThis air mass, or at least a portion of it, looks to hang around \nover the Midwest through the remainder of the period, with ensemble \nmeans showing only minor fluctuations in temperatures day-to-day. \n\nThere has understandably been a lot of attention on the mid-week \nperiod due to the Thanksgiving Holiday and different deterministic \ninitializations at times producing a strong surface system somewhere \nover the eastern half of the CONUS. Over the last several days, \nguidance has continued to converge on the overall pattern \nresponsible for this system. On Tuesday and Wednesday, a trough\nwill dig into the Central U.S., with a leading shortwave \ntracking along the Gulf Coast. It is this initial wave that is \nexpected to induce cyclogenesis over the Gulf states and track \nthe low northeastward, well east of the CWA. A second shortwave \nwill then dig southeastward over the Midwest Wednesday into \nThursday and phase with the first. It's this second wave that \nhas the best chance of producing light precipitation somewhere \nnear the CWA. That being said, the \"best\" chance right now for \nprecipitation locally is 10% or less across our Illinois \ncounties Wednesday afternoon and evening. Therefore, confidence \nis high in the CWA remaining impact-free through the holiday. \n\nDeterministic guidance shows additional waves traversing the \nbackside of the trough to end the week, but there is very little \nconsensus on the phasing of the trough by that point. At the very \nleast, a majority of guidance favors deep northwesterly flow over \nthe Middle Mississippi Valley, supporting local temperatures \nremaining at or below seasonal normals.",
